{"variant_id": "b809422c-0ced-4291-bd6f-c39d65e76e3e", "variant": "NC_000015.9:g.89859882C>T", "gene": "POLG", "disease": "mitochondrial disease", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM2"], "expert_panel": "Mitochondrial Diseases VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 59004, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning benign)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": [], "aiva_evidence": {"notable": "This is a 3' UTR variant (c.*100G>A) in POLG. ClinVar reports this variant as 'Uncertain significance' (VCV000886829). The variant is present in gnomAD at low frequency (AF=6.37e-05 in genomes, 7.47e-05 in exomes), which does not meet PM2 threshold for absence. As a 3' UTR variant, it does not affect protein coding sequence and is unlikely to have significant functional impact. POLG-related disorders are well-established autosomal recessive conditions, but pathogenic variants are typically missense or loss-of-function variants affecting the coding region. No functional studies or clinical evidence support pathogenicity of this specific 3' UTR variant."}}
{"variant_id": "20deabe7-61c1-4a9f-8370-4f08f79838bb", "variant": "NC_000021.8:g.36206722G>C", "gene": "RUNX1", "disease": "hereditary thrombocytopenia and hematologic cancer predisposition syndrome", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": [], "expert_panel": "Myeloid Malignancy VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 75712, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning pathogenic)", "aiva_confidence": "low", "aiva_criteria": ["PP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"PP4": "Patient phenotype (hereditary thrombocytopenia and hematologic cancer predisposition syndrome) is highly specific for RUNX1-related disease. RUNX1 is the primary gene associated with this condition (OMIM 601399), and the phenotype has a single genetic etiology.", "notable": "This missense variant (p.Gln264Glu) is located in the transactivation domain (TAD) of RUNX1, outside the critical Runt Homology Domain (RHD, aa 89-204). ClinVar classifies this as VUS. gnomAD shows extremely low frequency (AF=4.01e-06, 1 allele) but not complete absence, precluding PM2_supporting per RUNX1-specific rules. SIFT predicts deleterious_low_confidence (score 0.01), but REVEL score unavailable to apply PP3 per MM-VCEP guidelines (threshold \u22650.88). COSMIC reports this as a somatic variant (COSM6937056). PM1_supporting cannot be applied as position 264 is outside the RHD critical region (aa 89-204). No functional studies or segregation data available for this specific variant."}}
{"variant_id": "ba88029d-8764-4060-8a8f-aab28ad4e29b", "variant": "NC_000012.11:g.103237500G>C", "gene": "PAH", "disease": "phenylketonuria", "ground_truth": "Likely Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM3_Very Strong", "PP4_Moderate"], "expert_panel": "Phenylketonuria VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 63817, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Variant is rare in gnomAD: overall AF = 2.79e-05 (7/251,000 alleles in exomes), 1.31e-05 in genomes. For autosomal recessive PKU, this frequency is consistent with disease-causing variants. Meets PM2 threshold for extremely low frequency.", "PP3": "Computational evidence supports deleterious effect: SIFT = deleterious (0.01), PolyPhen = possibly damaging (0.905). Variant is listed in HGMD as disease mutation (CM1511453). Located in conserved catalytic domain (Pfam PF00351).", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (phenylketonuria) is highly specific for PAH deficiency. PKU has a well-established single genetic etiology with PAH being the primary causative gene. Clinical presentation strongly supports genetic diagnosis.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as 'Likely pathogenic' for Phenylketonuria (VCV000660581, RCV000817803). HGMD also lists this as a disease mutation (CM1511453).", "notable": "This is a missense variant (p.Gln375Glu) in the catalytic domain of PAH. The glutamine to glutamate substitution at position 375 is a conservative change but occurs in a functionally important region. No specific functional studies for this exact variant were identified in literature search. The variant is enriched in East Asian populations (AF ~0.0004) compared to other populations."}}
{"variant_id": "2a3b6394-60d8-451f-a60f-882a76f81607", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.45364521T>C", "gene": "ITGB3", "disease": "Glanzmann thrombasthenia", "ground_truth": "Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PP4_Strong", "PM3", "PM2_Supporting", "PP3", "PS3"], "expert_panel": "Platelet Disorders VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 88296, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PS3", "PM2", "PM3", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PS3": "Well-established functional studies (PMID:10891446) demonstrate damaging effect: Leu288Pro \u03b23 cDNA transfected into COS-7 cells showed delayed intracellular maturation and reduced surface expression. Transfected 293 cells with the variant were unable to bind immobilized fibrinogen, unlike WT cells which demonstrated significant adhesion and spreading. \u03b1IIb\u03b23 surface expression reduced to 10% of normal.", "PM2": "Absent from gnomAD v2.1.1 (allele frequency 0.00000). Variant is extremely rare, consistent with pathogenic variant for autosomal recessive Glanzmann thrombasthenia.", "PM3": "For this autosomal recessive disorder, variant was confirmed in trans with c.847_848del (classified Pathogenic by ClinGen PD VCEP) in Patient LD (PMID:10891446).", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: REVEL=0.99 (>0.75 threshold), SIFT=deleterious (score 0), PolyPhen=probably_damaging (score 0.999-1.0). Variant located in conserved Integrin beta chain domain (Pfam PF00362).", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (mucocutaneous bleeding, impaired platelet aggregation with all agonists except ristocetin, reduced \u03b1IIb\u03b23 expression) is highly specific for Glanzmann thrombasthenia, which has a well-established single genetic etiology (ITGB3 or ITGA2B mutations).", "PP5": "ClinGen Platelet Disorders Variant Curation Expert Panel classified this variant as Pathogenic (November 2022) with detailed evidence review. This is an FDA-recognized database with expert panel review status."}}
{"variant_id": "0b98e48e-6e33-4fb0-9e9f-7dcaac97c14f", "variant": "NC_000010.10:g.14976769T>G", "gene": "DCLRE1C", "disease": "severe combined immunodeficiency due to DCLRE1C deficiency", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "Severe Combined Immunodeficiency Disease  VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 69948, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning pathogenic)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2", "PP3", "PP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Variant is absent from gnomAD population databases. The variant (rs1839624547, chr10:g.14976769T>G) was not found in gnomAD, supporting rarity consistent with a pathogenic variant for this autosomal recessive disorder.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: SIFT=deleterious (score 0.01), PolyPhen=probably_damaging (score 0.951-0.995 across transcripts). The missense variant p.Lys157Thr affects a conserved residue in the metallo-\u03b2-lactamase domain of ARTEMIS protein, which is critical for nuclease activity.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (severe combined immunodeficiency due to DCLRE1C deficiency) is highly specific for DCLRE1C-related disease. SCID due to DCLRE1C deficiency (Artemis-SCID) is a well-characterized autosomal recessive disorder with a single genetic etiology. The T-B-NK+ SCID phenotype is characteristic of V(D)J recombination defects.", "notable": "ClinVar currently classifies this variant as VUS (VCV001013704). The variant affects position Lys157 in the metallo-\u03b2-lactamase/\u03b2-CASP domain, which is essential for V(D)J recombination and DNA damage repair. Loss-of-function is a well-established mechanism for DCLRE1C-related SCID (PMID:34220820). However, no functional studies specific to this missense variant (p.Lys157Thr) were identified, and no PS1/PM5 evidence exists for pathogenic variants at this exact position."}}
{"variant_id": "cbdd0d01-e0d5-481a-b07a-b7bdd4b84a54", "variant": "NC_000005.9:g.35873598G>T", "gene": "IL7R", "disease": "severe combined immunodeficiency, autosomal recessive, T cell-negative, B cell-positive, NK cell-positive", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "Severe Combined Immunodeficiency Disease  VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 661533, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "error": "HTTP 500: {\"detail\":{\"error\":{\"code\":\"INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR\",\"message\":\"Internal server error\",\"details\":\"Failed to complete variant classification\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-01-10T05:12:27.448121Z\",\"request_id\":\"0de01"}
{"variant_id": "d1ca3ef5-f2a9-4242-be3b-b59dff4af099", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.7576897G>T", "gene": "TP53", "disease": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome", "ground_truth": "Benign", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BP4", "BS3", "BS2", "PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "TP53 VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 65043, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Benign", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["BP4", "BP6"], "aiva_evidence": {"BP4": "Multiple computational tools predict benign effect: SIFT=tolerated (score 1.0), PolyPhen=benign (score 0.003-0.007). No CADD/DITTO scores available, but secondary predictors consistently indicate no damaging effect.", "BP6": "ClinVar reports this variant as Benign for Li-Fraumeni syndrome (RCV) and Likely benign for TP53-related disorder and Li-Fraumeni syndrome 1. Multiple submissions support benign classification.", "notable": "Variant is present in gnomAD at very low frequency (AF ~8.9e-06 exome, ~6.6e-06 genome), which is above the threshold for PM2 (not absent). Located in TP53 tetramerization domain (codon 317). ClinVar shows conflicting interpretations for hereditary cancer-predisposing syndrome, but consensus for Li-Fraumeni syndrome specifically is Benign/Likely benign. COSMIC entries (COSM10786, COSM43769) represent somatic occurrences, not germline pathogenicity evidence."}}
{"variant_id": "6abc06c2-c541-406d-ae14-32d9792848ca", "variant": "NC_000021.8:g.36259224C>T", "gene": "RUNX1", "disease": "hereditary thrombocytopenia and hematologic cancer predisposition syndrome", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BP4"], "expert_panel": "Myeloid Malignancy VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 441625, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning benign)", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["BP4", "BP7"], "aiva_evidence": {"BP4": "SpliceAI score = 0, indicating no predicted impact on splicing. This synonymous variant shows no computational evidence of deleterious effect. ClinGen Myeloid Malignancy VCEP applied BP4 in their expert panel review.", "BP7": "Synonymous variant (p.Leu89=) with no predicted splice impact. SpliceAI = 0 confirms no effect on canonical or cryptic splice sites.", "notable": "PM2 was considered but not applied per ClinGen VCEP guidelines for this gene. gnomAD frequency is extremely low (6.845e-07, ~1 allele in 1.5M). ClinVar has conflicting interpretations: Expert panel (VUS), Ambry Genetics (Likely Benign), and one somatic AML submission (Pathogenic, no criteria). The expert panel classification takes precedence. While RUNX1 LOF is a known disease mechanism (PVS1 applicable for null variants), this synonymous variant does not cause loss of function."}}
{"variant_id": "7390e608-04b8-42c9-b7d9-27c20be71622", "variant": "NC_000010.10:g.112724116C>T", "gene": "SHOC2", "disease": "RASopathy", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": [], "expert_panel": "RASopathy VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 63405, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PS3", "PS4", "PM1", "PM2", "PP2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PS3": "Well-established functional studies demonstrate gain-of-function mechanism. The p.Ser2Gly substitution introduces an N-myristoylation recognition motif causing constitutive targeting of SHOC2 to the plasma membrane and upregulation of RAS-MAPK signaling (PMID:19684605, PMID:35348676). In vitro studies show enhanced ERK phosphorylation and increased binding to MRAS and PPP1CB. In vivo zebrafish models demonstrate developmental defects including epiboly abnormalities, shortened body axis, heart looping defects, and craniofacial abnormalities.", "PS4": "This is the canonical recurrent pathogenic variant for Noonan syndrome-like disorder with loose anagen hair (Mazzanti syndrome). Multiple case reports and cohort studies document this variant in affected individuals across diverse populations (PMID:36579329, PMID:38019730, PMID:35348676). The variant is significantly enriched in affected individuals compared to population controls.", "PM1": "Located at position 2 (Ser2), a critical functional site. This position is essential for N-terminal processing and the p.Ser2Gly substitution creates an aberrant N-myristoylation site that fundamentally alters protein localization and function. The N-terminus of SHOC2 is a mutational hotspot with no benign variation observed.", "PM2": "Extremely rare in population databases. gnomAD genome shows AF=6.57\u00d710\u207b\u2076 (1 allele in ~152,000 alleles). This frequency is consistent with a rare pathogenic variant for an autosomal dominant disorder with high penetrance.", "PP2": "SHOC2 is a gene where missense variants are a well-established mechanism of disease. Pathogenic missense variants in SHOC2 cause Noonan syndrome-like disorder with loose anagen hair (RASopathy), and the gene has a low rate of benign missense variation.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect. SIFT=0.01 (deleterious), PolyPhen=0.833-0.919 (possibly/probably damaging across transcripts). The variant affects a highly conserved residue at the protein N-terminus critical for proper localization.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (RASopathy) is highly specific for SHOC2-related disorder. Noonan syndrome-like disorder with loose anagen hair has a distinctive phenotype including characteristic facial features, loose anagen hair, cardiac defects, and short stature, with SHOC2 being the primary causative gene.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Pathogenic with multiple submissions (VCV000006821, rs267607048). Multiple RCV records classify as Pathogenic for Noonan syndrome-like disorder with loose anagen hair 1, RASopathy, and Noonan syndrome. Also listed in HGMD (CM095445) and COSMIC (COSV54624833)."}}
{"variant_id": "c1e74a9b-b014-42e8-a917-476e4337fb7a", "variant": "NC_000001.10:g.171605076A>G", "gene": "MYOC", "disease": "juvenile open angle glaucoma", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PP1_Moderate", "PS3_Moderate", "PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "Glaucoma VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 81945, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning pathogenic)", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PS3_Moderate", "PP1_Moderate", "PM2_Supporting"], "aiva_evidence": {"PS3_Moderate": "Functional study (PMID:16466712) demonstrated that Ser502Pro protein has reduced secretion levels compared to wild-type myocilin, indicating impaired protein function. This meets the OddsPath threshold (>4.3) for PS3_Moderate per ClinGen Glaucoma VCEP specifications.", "PP1_Moderate": "Co-segregation with juvenile open angle glaucoma (JOAG) in 1 family with 6 meioses reported (PMID:9863594). This meets the PP1_Moderate threshold (5-6 meioses) per ClinGen Glaucoma VCEP specifications.", "PM2_Supporting": "Absent from all populations in gnomAD v2.1.1, meeting the \u22640.0001 frequency threshold for PM2_Supporting in a population of at least 10,000 alleles. Variant is extremely rare.", "notable": "ClinGen Glaucoma Variant Curation Expert Panel classified this variant as VUS (May 2022) with a score of 5 (VUS range: -1 to 5). REVEL score of 0.657 did not meet the \u22650.7 threshold for PP3 per VCEP specifications. SIFT predicts deleterious (0.01) and PolyPhen predicts possibly damaging (0.635). Variant is located in the olfactomedin domain, a critical functional region. Only 1 proband reported, not meeting the \u22652 probands threshold for PS4_Supporting."}}
{"variant_id": "55c3283e-f0f6-4e18-b837-99a275ea7d90", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.7577539G>A", "gene": "TP53", "disease": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome", "ground_truth": "Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PS3", "PS2", "PS4", "PP1_Strong", "PP3_Moderate", "PP4_Moderate", "PM1", "PM5", "PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "TP53 VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 55292, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PS1", "PS3", "PS4", "PM1", "PM2", "PP2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PS1": "Same amino acid change (p.Arg248Trp) as previously established pathogenic variant. ClinVar VCV000012347 reports this exact variant as Pathogenic for Li-Fraumeni syndrome with multiple submitters (allele_id: 27386).", "PS3": "Well-established functional studies demonstrate damaging effect. CIViC evidence shows dominant-negative function (EID10261, PMID:11896595), oncogenicity (EID7120, PMID:8099841), and gain-of-function properties. Multiple publications confirm loss of DNA-binding and transcriptional activity.", "PS4": "Variant significantly enriched in affected individuals. ClinVar shows pathogenic classifications for Li-Fraumeni syndrome, hereditary cancer-predisposing syndrome, breast/ovarian cancer, gastric cancer, and multiple other malignancies. COSMIC reports as recurrent somatic mutation (COSM10656, COSM11564, COSM44920).", "PM1": "Located in mutational hotspot within the TP53 DNA-binding domain. Codon 248 is one of the most frequently mutated positions in TP53, directly contacting DNA. VEP shows protein_protein_contact effect and location in critical functional domain (Pfam PF00870).", "PM2": "Extremely rare in population databases. gnomAD exome AF = 3.97652e-06 (approximately 1 allele in 251,000 individuals). This frequency is consistent with a highly penetrant pathogenic variant for a dominant disorder.", "PP2": "Missense variant in TP53, a gene with low rate of benign missense variation and where pathogenic missense variants are a common mechanism of disease. TP53 is highly constrained with missense variants being the predominant pathogenic mechanism in Li-Fraumeni syndrome.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect. SIFT = deleterious (score 0), PolyPhen = probably_damaging (score 1.0). Variant affects critical DNA-binding residue with complete loss of function predicted.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) is highly specific for TP53 germline pathogenic variants. LFS has a single genetic etiology with >95% of cases caused by TP53 mutations.", "PP5": "Multiple reputable sources report as pathogenic. ClinVar: Pathogenic with multiple submitters and review status. OMIM: 191170.0001. CIViC: Oncogenic with functional evidence."}}
{"variant_id": "073335d6-5612-4a86-a192-a78286e4e9c9", "variant": "NC_000001.10:g.173881032G>A", "gene": "SERPINC1", "disease": "antithrombin III deficiency", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BS1", "PP3"], "expert_panel": "Thrombosis VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 60364, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning benign)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PP3", "PP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"PP3": "SIFT=deleterious (score 0), PolyPhen=probably_damaging (score 1.0). Multiple computational tools predict damaging effect on protein function.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (antithrombin III deficiency) is highly specific for SERPINC1 mutations. SERPINC1 is the only known gene causing hereditary antithrombin deficiency.", "notable": "ClinVar classifies as Uncertain Significance (VCV000529744). Variant is present in gnomAD at low frequency (AF=0.00012-0.00041), which is higher than expected for a fully penetrant pathogenic variant in a rare disease. PM2 not met due to presence in population databases. No functional studies or case reports specific to p.Arg177Cys were identified."}}
{"variant_id": "76c296db-384a-40a6-b5d5-b5e7fd87091b", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.45387518T>C", "gene": "ITGB3", "disease": "Glanzmann thrombasthenia", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM2_Supporting", "PM3_Supporting", "PP4_Moderate"], "expert_panel": "Platelet Disorders VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 130785, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PVS1", "PM2", "PS3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PVS1": "Although annotated as synonymous (p.Gly477=), functional studies (PMID:36704147, 37716584) demonstrate this variant creates a cryptic splice donor site causing 95bp deletion and frameshift (p.G477Afs*30), resulting in a null allele. ITGB3 loss-of-function is an established mechanism for Glanzmann thrombasthenia (autosomal recessive). PVS1 applies as this effectively functions as a null variant.", "PM2": "Absent from gnomAD population database. Not found in any population controls. For autosomal recessive disorder, absence from controls strongly supports pathogenicity.", "PS3": "Well-established functional studies demonstrate pathogenicity: (1) RT-PCR and cDNA sequencing confirmed aberrant splicing with 95bp deletion (PMID:36704147); (2) Flow cytometry showed near-absent CD41/CD61 expression on patient platelets; (3) Blood clot retraction test showed no clot shrinkage over 24h; (4) Additional functional study (PMID:37716584) confirmed splicing defect and reduced ITGB3 protein expression.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (Glanzmann thrombasthenia) is highly specific for ITGB3/ITGA2B mutations. GT is a rare autosomal recessive disorder with single genetic etiology (\u03b1IIb\u03b23 integrin deficiency). Clinical features including mucocutaneous bleeding, absent clot retraction, and reduced CD41/CD61 expression are pathognomonic.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Pathogenic (Allele ID: 3401372) for Glanzmann thrombasthenia with supporting evidence from functional studies."}}
{"variant_id": "7ca8661b-1f2e-4d0c-882f-bfe8a40261db", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.26861383A>G", "gene": "FOXN1", "disease": "T-cell immunodeficiency, congenital alopecia, and nail dystrophy", "ground_truth": "Likely Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM1", "PP4", "PM2_Supporting", "PP3_Moderate"], "expert_panel": "Severe Combined Immunodeficiency Disease  VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 79570, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PM1", "PM2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM1": "Located in the forkhead (FKH) DNA-binding domain (Pfam PF00250, PROSITE PS50039) - a critical functional domain for FOXN1 transcription factor activity. Literature (PMID:33464451) demonstrates that mutations in the forkhead domain (e.g., R320W, V294I) directly affect protein-DNA interface and cause disease. The forkhead domain is essential for DNA binding and transcriptional regulation of thymic epithelial cell development.", "PM2": "Extremely rare in gnomAD exomes (AF = 6.841e-07, essentially 1 allele in ~1.5 million). Absent in gnomAD African, East Asian, South Asian, Finnish, Ashkenazi Jewish, and Latino populations. Meets PM2 threshold for rare variant in a semidominant disorder.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: SIFT = deleterious (score 0.0), PolyPhen-2 = probably_damaging (score 0.999). Both primary predictors strongly support pathogenicity.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (T-cell immunodeficiency, congenital alopecia, and nail dystrophy) is highly specific for FOXN1-related disease. This triad is pathognomonic for 'Nude SCID' caused by FOXN1 mutations, representing a single genetic etiology.", "PP5": "ClinVar submission (RCV000434343, VCV000392379) reports this variant as 'Likely pathogenic' for T-cell immunodeficiency, congenital alopecia, and nail dystrophy. One additional submission reports 'Uncertain significance' for 'not provided' condition.", "notable": "FOXN1 loss-of-function is an established disease mechanism (PMID:33464451, PMID:34860543). Functional studies demonstrate that forkhead domain mutations disrupt DNA binding and nuclear condensate formation essential for transcriptional activity. The variant affects a highly conserved histidine residue (H321) in the DNA-binding domain. Semidominant inheritance pattern is supported by literature showing heterozygous FOXN1 variants can cause immunodeficiency phenotypes."}}
{"variant_id": "4f439910-cad7-4dd9-a29d-ab86695a5962", "variant": "NC_000021.8:g.36164546G>T", "gene": "RUNX1", "disease": "hereditary thrombocytopenia and hematologic cancer predisposition syndrome", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BP4", "PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "Myeloid Malignancy VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 292920, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning pathogenic)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2", "PP2", "PP3", "PP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Absent in gnomAD v4.0 (0 alleles across all populations including gnomAD exomes: AFR=0, AMR=0, ASJ=0, EAS=0, FIN=0, MID=0, NFE=0, SAS=0). Meets PM2 threshold for rare variant in autosomal dominant disorder.", "PP2": "RUNX1 is a gene where missense variants are a known mechanism of disease. Germline RUNX1 mutations cause familial platelet disorder with predisposition to myeloid malignancy (FPD/AML). Both truncating and missense variants in critical domains are pathogenic.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: SIFT=deleterious (score 0), PolyPhen=probably_damaging (score 0.947). Variant located in conserved C-terminal transactivation domain (Pfam PF08504).", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (hereditary thrombocytopenia and hematologic cancer predisposition syndrome) is highly specific for RUNX1-related FPD/AML, which has a single genetic etiology with autosomal dominant inheritance.", "notable": "Variant is in the C-terminal transactivation domain where mutations have been shown to impair blood cell differentiation (PMID:38513139). ClinVar currently classifies as VUS. COSMIC reports this as a somatic variant (COSV55896509). No functional studies specific to p.Asn443Lys were identified."}}
{"variant_id": "f63de939-83d3-4928-aa91-593bd95d083c", "variant": "NC_000014.8:g.95590862G>T", "gene": "DICER1", "disease": "DICER1-related tumor predisposition", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BP4", "PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "DICER1 and miRNA-Processing Gene VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 73184, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning benign)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Variant is extremely rare in gnomAD: allele frequency 6.841e-07 in exomes and 6.571e-06 in genomes. Essentially absent from population controls, meeting PM2 threshold for rare variant.", "notable": "ClinVar classifies this variant as VUS (ClinVar allele ID: 642934). In-silico predictions are predominantly benign: SIFT=tolerated (0.05-0.27), PolyPhen=benign to possibly_damaging (0.033-0.986 depending on transcript). The variant is located at position 349, which is NOT within the RNase IIIa or RNase IIIb hotspot domains where pathogenic missense mutations typically cluster in DICER1. DICER1 pathogenicity typically involves germline truncating variants (loss of function) combined with somatic RNase III domain hotspot mutations (two-hit model). This missense variant outside critical domains with benign computational predictions does not fit the typical pathogenic pattern for DICER1."}}
{"variant_id": "c0326a24-f74a-4d2d-8623-525b5be1ae24", "variant": "NC_000012.11:g.121435307C>T", "gene": "HNF1A", "disease": "monogenic diabetes", "ground_truth": "Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PS4", "PP1_Strong", "PS2_Supporting", "PP4", "PP3", "PM2_Supporting", "PS3_Moderate"], "expert_panel": "Monogenic Diabetes VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 64842, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Benign", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["BP4", "BP7"], "aiva_evidence": {"BP4": "Synonymous variant with no predicted deleterious effect. VEP consequence is 'synonymous_variant' with LOW impact. No computational evidence supports a damaging effect on protein function.", "BP7": "Synonymous variant (c.909C>T, p.Ser303=) that does not alter the amino acid sequence. The variant is not located at a canonical splice site (\u00b11/2 positions) and VEP does not predict splice impact. No evidence of cryptic splice site activation.", "notable": "Variant is absent from gnomAD (supports rarity, PM2 consideration), but rarity alone does not indicate pathogenicity for a synonymous variant. COSMIC entry (COSV104568487) indicates somatic occurrence in cancer but does not support germline pathogenicity for MODY. HNF1A is a well-established MODY3 gene where loss-of-function is the disease mechanism, but synonymous variants typically do not cause loss of function unless they affect splicing."}}
{"variant_id": "7e5614a3-39a9-4c90-83ce-bb5af3514d80", "variant": "NC_000007.13:g.44185225G>A", "gene": "GCK", "disease": "monogenic diabetes", "ground_truth": "Likely Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PP4_Moderate", "PP2", "PP3", "PM2_Supporting", "PS4_Moderate"], "expert_panel": "Monogenic Diabetes VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 77859, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2", "PP2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Variant absent from gnomAD population database. No alleles observed in >140,000 individuals. Meets PM2 threshold for rare variant in a gene where heterozygous variants cause disease.", "PP2": "GCK is a gene where missense variants are a well-established mechanism of disease (GCK-MODY). The gene has a low rate of benign missense variation, and pathogenic missense variants are common in this gene. Over 600 GCK missense variants have been reported to cause MODY2.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: SIFT=deleterious (0.02), PolyPhen=probably_damaging (0.999). Variant affects conserved serine residue in the glucokinase catalytic domain (Pfam PF03727).", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (monogenic diabetes) is highly specific for GCK-MODY, which has a single genetic etiology. GCK-MODY accounts for 30-50% of all MODY cases and presents with mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia from birth.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Likely Pathogenic (VCV000036175) with 3 independent submissions for GCK-MODY/MODY2 and monogenic diabetes. Multiple clinical laboratories have classified this variant consistently.", "notable": "Comprehensive deep mutational scanning study (PMID: 37101203) provides functional activity scores for nearly all GCK variants with strong correlation to clinical phenotypes. The variant is located in a structurally important region of the glucokinase domain. PubMed citations (32375122, 29510678, 27269892, 30257192, 33129248, 31197960) are associated with this variant in ClinVar."}}
{"variant_id": "95e42e40-98b1-4795-857e-f2677975ddd4", "variant": "NC_000003.11:g.10183620G>A", "gene": "VHL", "disease": "von Hippel-Lindau disease", "ground_truth": "Likely Benign", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BS2_Supporting", "BS1"], "expert_panel": "VHL VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 62801, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Benign", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["BS3", "BP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"BS3": "Saturation genome editing study (PMID: 38969834) systematically assayed nearly all VHL SNVs. The study found that no variants in the N-terminal region before p.M54 (including p.Gly30) scored as depleted/loss-of-function. This is because VHL has an alternative translation initiation site at p.M54 that produces a fully functional isoform, making the first 53 amino acids dispensable for tumor suppressor function. This constitutes well-established functional evidence showing no damaging effect.", "BP4": "SIFT prediction is tolerated_low_confidence (0.15). While PolyPhen predicts probably_damaging (0.934), the functional SGE data directly contradicts computational predictions for this region, demonstrating that computational tools lack specificity for variants in the dispensable N-terminal region.", "notable": "ClinVar shows conflicting interpretations (VUS/Likely benign). gnomAD frequency is very low (AF ~3.5e-05) but not absent, which is consistent with a benign or low-penetrance variant. The variant is located in a region (before p.M54) where no pathogenic variants have been reported in ClinVar, consistent with the functional dispensability of this region."}}
{"variant_id": "72b8fac7-0135-48cb-be40-e9f09ed1f4b4", "variant": "NC_000007.13:g.44186061C>T", "gene": "GCK", "disease": "monogenic diabetes", "ground_truth": "Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PP4_Moderate", "PVS1", "PM2_Supporting", "PS4"], "expert_panel": "Monogenic Diabetes VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 58182, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PVS1", "PM2", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PVS1": "Canonical splice donor variant (c.1019+1G>A) at +1 position in GCK gene. Loss-of-function is a well-established mechanism for MODY2/GCK-MODY. Heterozygous LOF variants cause haploinsufficiency leading to mild fasting hyperglycemia. VEP confirms HIGH impact splice_donor_variant across multiple transcripts (ENST00000345378, ENST00000395796, ENST00000403799, ENST00000437084).", "PM2": "Variant absent from gnomAD population database. No allele frequency data found in myvariant or VEP colocated variants, consistent with rare pathogenic variant.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (monogenic diabetes) is highly specific for GCK-MODY (MODY2), which has a single genetic etiology. GCK mutations account for 30-50% of MODY cases and present with characteristic mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Pathogenic (Allele ID: 2831815) for monogenic diabetes. HGMD also catalogues this variant (CS052962) as a disease-causing mutation."}}
{"variant_id": "12985d15-e1e7-45ab-b3e5-48e082086d4d", "variant": "NC_000010.10:g.89685314T>C", "gene": "PTEN", "disease": "PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome", "ground_truth": "Likely Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM2", "PP2", "PS4_Supporting", "PS3"], "expert_panel": "PTEN VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 148700, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PM1", "PM2", "PP2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM1": "Located in the PTEN phosphatase domain (residue 70), a critical functional domain. VEP shows multiple protein domain annotations including Gene3D, Pfam (PF22785), SMART (SM00404), and CDD (cd14509). The phosphatase domain (residues 7-185) is essential for PTEN tumor suppressor function and is a known mutational hotspot.", "PM2": "Absent from gnomAD population database. No allele frequency data found in myvariant or VEP colocated variants. For a rare autosomal dominant disorder like PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome, absence from population databases supports pathogenicity.", "PP2": "PTEN is a well-established tumor suppressor gene where missense variants are a common mechanism of disease. The gene has a low rate of benign missense variation, and pathogenic missense variants are frequently reported in Cowden syndrome and PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: SIFT = deleterious (score 0.0), PolyPhen = probably_damaging (score 1.0). The Leu70Pro substitution introduces a proline residue which disrupts protein secondary structure in the phosphatase domain.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome) is highly specific for PTEN gene mutations. PHTS is caused almost exclusively by germline PTEN mutations, making this a single-gene etiology condition.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Pathogenic/Likely Pathogenic (VCV000007826, allele_id 22865) with multiple submissions for Cowden syndrome 1, PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome, hereditary cancer-predisposing syndrome, and related conditions. HGMD entries (CM1513158, CS982332) also support pathogenicity. PMID:9832031 cited as supporting evidence.", "notable": "COSMIC database contains multiple somatic entries (COSV64310663/COSM5108, COSV64301989/COSM3397283, COSV107457738/COSM10286964) indicating this variant is recurrently observed in cancer samples, further supporting its functional impact. UniProt annotation VAR_018102 also documents this variant."}}
{"variant_id": "63ca0364-f7f7-4ed4-8127-18f4feff245f", "variant": "NC_000023.10:g.149809827C>T", "gene": "MTM1", "disease": "centronuclear myopathy", "ground_truth": "Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PP3", "PM2_Supporting", "PS3_Moderate", "PS4", "PM6"], "expert_panel": "Congenital Myopathies VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 54799, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PVS1", "PM2", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PVS1": "Nonsense variant (p.Arg421Ter) creating a premature stop codon in MTM1, a gene where loss of function is an established mechanism of disease for X-linked myotubular myopathy/centronuclear myopathy. The variant affects multiple protein-coding transcripts with HIGH impact, resulting in truncation of the myotubularin protein within critical functional domains (Pfam PF06602, PROSITE PS51339, SMART SM00404).", "PM2": "Extremely rare in population databases. gnomAD exome shows AF = 5.46e-06 (1 allele in ~183,000 total alleles). This frequency is consistent with a rare pathogenic variant for an X-linked recessive disorder.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (centronuclear myopathy) is highly specific for MTM1-related disease. X-linked centronuclear myopathy (myotubular myopathy) has a well-established single genetic etiology with MTM1 mutations. The clinical presentation matches the expected phenotype.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Pathogenic (VCV000158913) with multiple independent submissions (RCV000146392, RCV000724986, RCV003389044, RCV004017420) for severe X-linked myotubular myopathy and centronuclear myopathy. Supporting literature includes PMIDs: 18414213, 9285787, 9305655."}}
{"variant_id": "04430f0e-93d8-4407-9ac9-c6268cb1dbba", "variant": "NC_000003.11:g.12626130T>C", "gene": "RAF1", "disease": "RASopathy", "ground_truth": "Benign", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BA1"], "expert_panel": "RASopathy VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 50922, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning benign)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["BP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"BP4": "Deep intronic variant (c.-26-7383C>G) located 7383 bp from nearest splice site. VEP predicts 'intron_variant' with MODIFIER impact. No predicted effect on splicing or protein function. Computational evidence suggests no deleterious impact.", "notable": "PM2 was considered but not applied: While variant is absent from gnomAD, deep intronic variants are typically underrepresented in population databases and absence alone does not support pathogenicity for non-coding variants. RAF1-associated RASopathies are caused by gain-of-function missense mutations affecting protein function, not intronic variants. No ClinVar entry exists for this variant."}}
{"variant_id": "c6b044c8-0623-4882-8baa-17bfb6a3ffd6", "variant": "NC_000007.13:g.107340607G>A", "gene": "SLC26A4", "disease": "Pendred syndrome", "ground_truth": "Likely Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PS3_Supporting", "PP4", "PM2_Supporting", "PM3_Strong"], "expert_panel": "Hearing Loss VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 64434, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Extremely rare in gnomAD: genome AF=3.19e-05 (1 allele), exome AF=9.38e-06. Absent in EAS, AFR, AMR, FIN, ASJ, SAS populations. Meets PM2 threshold for autosomal recessive disorder.", "PP3": "SIFT predicts deleterious (score 0.02-0.04 across transcripts). PolyPhen ranges from benign (0.308-0.317) to possibly damaging (0.458). Missense at conserved cysteine residue (Cys565Tyr) in functional Sulfate_transp domain (Pfam PF01740). CADD/DITTO not available but SIFT supports deleterious effect.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (Pendred syndrome) is highly specific for SLC26A4-related disease. Pendred syndrome has a well-defined genetic etiology with SLC26A4 being the primary causative gene.", "PP5": "ClinVar reports this variant as Likely pathogenic/Pathogenic (VCV000043519) with multiple submissions: Pathogenic for DFNB4, Likely pathogenic for Pendred syndrome and rare genetic deafness. 22 PubMed citations support pathogenicity (PMIDs: 9618166, 11317356, 14508505, 14679580, 15689455, 15720248, 16570074, 16950989, 19040761, 19204907, 19578036, 19620588, 20826203, 22116358, 22384008, 24033266, 24599119, 26346818, 28341401, 31599023, 33801843).", "notable": "This variant affects a conserved cysteine residue at position 565 within the Sulfate_transp domain (Pfam PF01740). The C565Y substitution introduces a bulky tyrosine in place of cysteine, likely disrupting protein structure/function. SLC26A4 encodes pendrin, an anion exchanger critical for inner ear and thyroid function. Loss of function is the established disease mechanism for Pendred syndrome (autosomal recessive). HGMD ID: CM981511 also catalogues this variant."}}
{"variant_id": "5160e951-527b-4568-9c40-fcdc1f3ea0a5", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.45360916G>A", "gene": "ITGB3", "disease": "Glanzmann thrombasthenia", "ground_truth": "Pathogenic", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM2_Supporting", "PVS1", "PP4_Moderate"], "expert_panel": "Platelet Disorders VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 71941, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PVS1", "PM2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PVS1": "Canonical splice donor variant (c.361+1G>A) at the +1 position of intron 3 in ITGB3. Predicted to cause skipping of exon 3, resulting in frameshift (p.Ala56Metfs*23) and nonsense-mediated decay. Loss-of-function is an established disease mechanism for Glanzmann thrombasthenia (autosomal recessive). ClinGen Platelet Disorders VCEP confirmed PVS1 applicability.", "PM2": "Variant is absent from gnomAD v2.1.1 controls. Extremely rare in gnomAD exomes (frequency 0.00000). For autosomal recessive disorder, this meets PM2 threshold (applied at supporting level per ClinGen VCEP specifications).", "PP3": "Computational evidence strongly supports splice site disruption. Variant occurs at canonical GT dinucleotide of splice donor site. Multiple splice prediction algorithms predict loss of donor site function.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (mucocutaneous bleeding with impaired platelet aggregation to all agonists except ristocetin, and \u03b23 surface expression reduced to 0.61%) is highly specific for Glanzmann thrombasthenia, a disorder with single genetic etiology (ITGA2B or ITGB3). ClinGen VCEP applied PP4 at moderate strength.", "PP5": "ClinGen Platelet Disorders Variant Curation Expert Panel classified this variant as Pathogenic (reviewed by expert panel, September 2021). This is an FDA-recognized database with detailed evidence review. Labcorp Genetics also classified as Likely Pathogenic with supporting literature (PMID:32237906, 21917754, 16199547)."}}
{"variant_id": "899e1465-0b1f-4c76-a4e9-f9669ff0edfd", "variant": "NC_000017.10:g.7124941G>A", "gene": "ACADVL", "disease": "very long chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["PM3_Supporting", "PP4_Moderate", "PP3", "PM2_Supporting"], "expert_panel": "ACADVL VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 79477, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning pathogenic)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": ["PM2", "PP3", "PP4"], "aiva_evidence": {"PM2": "Variant is absent from gnomAD population database (rs2071231356 has no frequency data). For autosomal recessive disorders like VLCADD, absence from population databases supports PM2 at moderate level.", "PP3": "Multiple computational tools predict deleterious effect: SIFT=deleterious (score 0), PolyPhen=probably_damaging (score 0.995-1.0). The glycine to serine substitution at position 188 occurs within the conserved acyl-CoA dehydrogenase domain (Pfam PF02771). Note: CADD and DITTO scores not available in annotation data.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (very long chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) is highly specific for ACADVL-related disease. VLCADD has a single genetic etiology (ACADVL gene) with autosomal recessive inheritance pattern.", "notable": "ClinVar currently classifies this variant as VUS (VCV000838654). The variant affects a glycine residue in the catalytic domain, and glycine residues are often structurally important. An ACADVL-specific VCEP has published guidelines (PMID: 37549443) for variant interpretation. No functional studies or case reports specifically describing this variant were identified. For definitive classification, functional enzyme activity studies or identification in trans with a known pathogenic variant would be valuable."}}
{"variant_id": "9c35fc4f-823c-4133-b23b-d26a27d622e1", "variant": "NC_000019.9:g.39006807A>G", "gene": "RYR1", "disease": "malignant hyperthermia of anesthesia", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": [], "expert_panel": "Malignant Hyperthermia Susceptibility VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 697880, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "VUS (leaning benign)", "aiva_confidence": "medium", "aiva_criteria": [], "aiva_evidence": {"notable": "This RYR1 missense variant (p.Glu3212Gly) has conflicting ClinVar interpretations (VUS for MH, Likely Benign for other RYR1-related disorders). The variant is present in gnomAD at low frequency (AF ~0.00026-0.00041), which is below the RYR1/MH-specific BA1 (0.38%) and BS1 (0.08%) thresholds but above the PM2 threshold. Per the ClinGen RYR1/MH Expert Panel guidelines, PM2 is NOT applicable for RYR1/MH classification. The variant is located at position 3212, which falls OUTSIDE all three defined MH hotspot regions (N-terminal 1-552, central 2101-2458, C-terminal 4631-4991), so PM1 does not apply. In silico predictions are conflicting: SIFT predicts deleterious (0.03) while PolyPhen predicts benign (0.318). No REVEL or CADD scores were available from the annotation. No functional studies or case reports specific to this variant were identified in the literature. The variant has not been classified as pathogenic by the European Malignant Hyperthermia Group."}}
{"variant_id": "21f25196-ecca-494f-bb6c-53ab02e41537", "variant": "NC_000001.10:g.115256461T>C", "gene": "NRAS", "disease": "RASopathy", "ground_truth": "Uncertain Significance", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BP4"], "expert_panel": "RASopathy VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 78120, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Pathogenic", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["PS1", "PM1", "PM2", "PP2", "PP3", "PP4", "PP5"], "aiva_evidence": {"PS1": "Same amino acid change (p.Gly12Ser) as previously established pathogenic variant. ClinVar reports this exact variant (rs121913250, c.34G>A) as Pathogenic for Noonan syndrome 6, RASopathy, and JMML with multiple submissions (RCV000158979, RCV000158985, RCV000212761). The G12S substitution at this position has been independently classified as pathogenic.", "PM1": "Located in a critical functional domain - codon 12 is a well-established mutational hotspot in the GTPase domain of NRAS. Mutations at codons 12, 13, and 61 are known to impair GTPase activity and cause constitutive RAS activation. The variant falls within the P-loop (phosphate-binding loop) which is essential for GTP hydrolysis. Multiple protein domains identified: Ras family (PF00071), Gene3D, SMART domains (SM00173-175).", "PM2": "Extremely rare in population databases. gnomAD exomes v2.1 shows frequency of 2.737e-06 (approximately 1 in 365,000 alleles), with only 1 allele observed in Ashkenazi Jewish population (3.826e-05). This is well below the threshold expected for a dominant disorder with high penetrance.", "PP2": "NRAS is a gene where missense variants are a common mechanism of disease. Germline activating mutations in NRAS cause Noonan syndrome 6 (autosomal dominant), and the gene has a low rate of benign missense variation. Pathogenic missense variants at codons 12, 13, and 61 are well-documented.", "PP3": "Computational evidence supports deleterious effect. SIFT score = 0.02 (deleterious). The variant affects a highly conserved glycine residue in the P-loop that is critical for GTPase function. Multiple COSMIC entries (COSV54736487, COSV54736621, COSV54736940) indicate somatic occurrence in cancer, supporting functional impact.", "PP4": "Patient phenotype (RASopathy) is highly specific for germline mutations in RAS-MAPK pathway genes. NRAS is one of the established genes causing Noonan syndrome 6, which is part of the RASopathy spectrum. The phenotype-genotype correlation is strong.", "PP5": "ClinVar classifies this variant as Pathogenic with multiple independent submissions. Clinical significance allele annotation: 'T:pathogenic;A:pathogenic;G:pathogenic' for all nucleotide changes at this position. Multiple PMIDs support pathogenicity (PMID:2278970, 2674680, 3122217, 8120410, 12460918, 16273091, and many others)."}}
{"variant_id": "bdfc25cc-1f68-446b-8bf9-541b88b4a45a", "variant": "NC_000023.10:g.153296132G>A", "gene": "MECP2", "disease": "Rett syndrome", "ground_truth": "Likely Benign", "ground_truth_criteria": ["BP5", "BS2"], "expert_panel": "Rett and Angelman-like Disorders VCEP", "execution_time_ms": 79281, "model_used": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5-20251101", "aiva_classification": "Likely Benign", "aiva_confidence": "high", "aiva_criteria": ["BS1", "BP4", "BP6"], "aiva_evidence": {"BS1": "Variant present in gnomAD at allele frequency ~2.7\u00d710\u207b\u2076 to 8.9\u00d710\u207b\u2076. While rare, this frequency is higher than expected for a fully penetrant X-linked dominant disorder like Rett syndrome, where pathogenic variants are typically absent or extremely rare in population databases.", "BP4": "SIFT predicts 'tolerated' (score 0.05). The variant is a conservative missense change (Pro\u2192Ser) that does not introduce a stop codon or frameshift. No CADD or DITTO scores available, but SIFT prediction supports benign effect.", "BP6": "ClinVar classifies this variant as 'Likely benign' (VCV001189793) with multiple submissions for MECP2-related disorder, severe neonatal-onset encephalopathy with microcephaly, and 'not provided' conditions. Clinical significance allele annotation confirms likely benign status.", "notable": "This missense variant (p.Pro176Ser/p.Pro188Ser) occurs outside the critical methyl-DNA binding domain (MBD) and transcription repressor domain (TRD) of MECP2. The Ehrhart et al. 2021 catalogue of 863 RTT-causing MECP2 mutations does not list this variant among pathogenic variants. While MECP2 loss-of-function causes Rett syndrome, this specific missense change appears to be tolerated based on population frequency and computational predictions."}}
